<p>
    <em>
        Assessing the Impact of Community Rehabilitation Provider (CRP) Characteristics and Service Delivery Models on
        Employment
    </em>
</p>

<h2>About the CRP Project</h2>

<p>
    How do CRP characteristics and service delivery models influence competitive integrated employment (CIE) outcomes
    for people with intellectual and developmental disabilities (IDD)?
</p>

<p>
    <a href="https://communityinclusion.org">ICI/UMass Boston</a> is conducting this project in partnership with the <a
        href="https://ancor.org">
        American Network of Community Options and Resources (ANCOR).
    </a>
</p>

<p>We seek to understand:</p>

<ul>
    <li>what CRP characteristics support &amp; improve CIE outcomes</li>
    <li>how CRP characteristics, service delivery practices, and state-level factors influence CIE outcomes</li>
    <li>how to strengthen service systems across states and provider types</li>
</ul>

<aside class="bg_ici_lightgrey card card-body mb-4">
    <h3>What is Competitive Integrated Employment (CIE)?</h3>
    <p>Full or part-time work where a person with a disability:</p>
    <ul class="">
        <li class="">is paid at least minimum wage</li>
        <li class="">
            is paid the same as co-workers without disabilities who have similar training, experience, and job duties
        </li>
        <li class="">gets the same benefits as other co-workers without disabilities</li>
        <li class="">is working alongside people with and without disabilities</li>
        <li class="">
            has the same opportunities to advance at work and get promotions as co-workers without disabilities
        </li>
    </ul>
</aside>

<p>
    A national Advisory Board with researchers, practitioners, service providers, policymakers, individuals with IDD,
    and family members guides the CRP Project.
</p>

<h3>What We Will Do</h3>

<p>Using a mixed-methods research approach, we will conduct three studies:</p>
<ul>
    <li>
        <strong><a href="#study-1">Study 1: National CRP Survey</a></strong> — A national survey of CRPs to study provider
        characteristics, service delivery models, workforce practices, technology use, and strategies for adapting to changing
        service and policy contexts
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong><a href="#study-2">Study 2: Integrated Data Analysis</a></strong> — Analyses combining CRP survey data with
        state-level employment, economic, and policy data
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong><a href="#study-3">Study 3: High-Performing CRP Case Studies</a></strong> — Case studies of high-performing
        CRPs to identify effective and scalable practices associated with CIE outcomes
    </li>
</ul>

<p>
    With the knowledge we gain from our research, we will develop practical, evidence-based recommendations for
    providers, policymakers, and others who have a vested interest in this work.
</p>

<p>
    Explore each study &amp; sign up for our <a href="https://lp.constantcontactpages.com/sl/VAsWN3m/ici">
        ICI Email List
    </a> to receive updates on products we create from what we learn.
</p>

<h3>Why This Project Matters</h3>
<Image
    src={ph2}
    alt="A woman wearing headphones works on a laptop in an office"
    class="img-fluid float-end ms-3 mb-3 img-fluid rounded"
    style="max-width: 400px;"
/>
<p>
    CRPs are the main providers of employment and non-work services to people with IDD, who make up more than 80% of the
    people they serve.
</p>

<p>There are thousands of CRPs across the United States, and most provide both employment and non-work services.</p>

<h4>CRPs face major challenges:</h4>
<ul>
    <li>We don't have enough current national data on CRPs, and the data we do have is fragmented.</li>
    <li>We don't know the capacity of smaller CRPs.</li>
    <li>Federal and state policy goals do not align with CRP service models.</li>
    <li>Service systems are complex, which makes coordination, funding, and service delivery more complicated.</li>
    <li>CRPs face workforce &amp; funding shortages.</li>
</ul>
<section id="study-1">
    <h1>Study 1: National CRP Survey</h1>

    <p>
        Building on more than 40 years of CRP survey research by ICI, Study 1 will contribute updated evidence on CRP
        capacity and practices in the current service and policy environment.
    </p>

    <p>
        <strong>From July–September 2026, we are surveying CRPs across the US about their:</strong>
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>organizational characteristics</li>
        <li>service delivery, trends, and strategic priorities</li>
        <li>budgets and funding sources</li>
        <li>workforce characteristics</li>
        <li>remote technologies in employment services</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>How We Reach CRPs for the Survey</h3>

    <p>We will draw from multiple data sources:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>ANCOR's membership list, with more than 2,500 CRPs all across the US</li>
        <li>State Employment Leadership Network (SELN) members from across 28 state IDD agencies</li>
        <li>Additional state IDD agencies</li>
        <li>State vocational rehabilitation (VR) agencies</li>
        <li>State provider association websites</li>
        <li>US Department of Labor Section 14(c) certificate holder listings</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Together, these sources form a national sampling frame of approximately 11,300 CRPs.</p>

    <h3>What We Will Produce</h3>

    <p>With the knowledge we gain from our survey research, we will:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>provide an updated, comprehensive assessment of CRP capacity</li>
        <li>identify the organizational characteristics and practices associated with stronger CIE outcomes</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
        <strong>Want to stay informed?</strong><br />
        Sign up for the <a href="https://lp.constantcontactpages.com/sl/VAsWN3m/ici">ICI email list</a> to receive updates
        and the following products:
        <ul>
            <li>Two peer-reviewed journal articles</li>
            <li>A research report summarizing survey findings</li>
            <li>A plain-language version of the summary report</li>
            <li>Two Research-to-Practice briefs</li>
            <li>Infographics and other visual products to disseminate key findings</li>
        </ul>
    </p>
</section>
<section id="study-2">
    <h1>Study 2: Integrated Data Analysis</h1>

    <p>
        How do CRP characteristics, service delivery practices, and state-level factors influence employment outcomes
        for people with IDD?
    </p>

    <p>
        Building on Study 1, we will use the CRP Survey dataset as well as state-level employment, economic, and policy
        data to study relationships among CRP characteristics, service delivery practices, state-level contextual
        factors, and employment outcomes.
    </p>

    <h3>What We Will Study</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Whether Employment First policies are in place</li>
        <li>Adoption of Technology First initiatives</li>
        <li>Whether subminimum wage employment (Section 14(c)) has been eliminated</li>
        <li>Minimum wage levels relative to federal minimum wage</li>
        <li>Whether a state participates in the State Employment Leadership Network (SELN), and for how long</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>What We Will Produce</h3>

    <p>This analysis will identify key factors associated with CIE outcomes across CRPs and states.</p>
    <p>
        <strong>Want to stay informed?</strong><br />
        Sign up for the <a href="https://lp.constantcontactpages.com/sl/VAsWN3m/ici">ICI email list</a> to receive updates
        and the following products:
        <ul>
            <li>A peer-reviewed journal article</li>
            <li>A plain-language version and an easy-read summary of predictors of CIE outcomes</li>
            <li>Two Research-to-Practice briefs</li>
            <li>A webinar for the CRP community</li>
        </ul>
    </p>
</section>
<section id="study-3">
    <h1>Study 3: Case Studies of High-Performing CRPs</h1>

    <p>
        We will review documents and conduct in-depth interviews with six high-performing CRPs to explore what makes
        them successful:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            What strategies do high-performing CRPs use to transform employment service delivery and expand access to
            CIE?
        </li>
        <li>What factors contribute to effective CIE service delivery?</li>
    </ul>

    <p>We will pay particular attention to factors of workforce development and technology use.</p>

    <h3>How We Identify Who is "High Performing"</h3>

    <p>
        We will study CRPs that support people with IDD in obtaining, maintaining, and advancing in individual CIE. We
        will identify them based on evidence-based indicators established through Studies 1 and 2, as well as findings
        from prior research.
    </p>

    <p>
        <strong>Some indicators that we consider when identifying high-performing CRPs include:</strong>
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>percentage of individuals with IDD working for pay in CIE</li>
        <li>percentage of CRP staff providing CIE services</li>
        <li>presence of nationally recognized certification in employment services</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>What We Will Produce</h3>

    <p>
        Building on more than 15 years of ICI research documenting promising practices among CRPs, and grounded in the <em>
            <a href="https://www.thinkwork.org/promising-practices/service-provider">
                Ten Elements of Provider Transformation
            </a>
        </em>, this study will contribute updated evidence on how high-performing CRPs operationalize effective service
        delivery in practice.
    </p>

    <p>
        <strong>Want to stay informed?</strong><br />
        Sign up for the <a href="https://lp.constantcontactpages.com/sl/VAsWN3m/ici">ICI email list</a> to receive updates
        and the following products:
        <ul>
            <li>A peer-reviewed journal article</li>
            <li>Six CRP case studies highlighting effective CIE strategies</li>
            <li>Two Research-to-Practice briefs focused on workforce development and technology use</li>
        </ul>
    </p>
</section>
